ght be forced to  pay out-of-state tuition on top of that  no financial aid and this could possibly  mean you're not being able to anymore go  to that school or program you know as  undocumented folks you can't really  think of anything in long term there's  never really been a sense of stability  and sometimes it's hard to invest  yourselves because in my mind I never  know like in five years in a year in  couple months where will I be so it's  hard to plan ahead for life and it's  hard to just work towards something  [Music]  this morning Secretary Napolitano  announced new actions my administration  will take to mend our nation's  immigration policy to make it more fair  more efficient and more just  specifically for certain young people  sometimes called dreamers now these are  young people who study in our schools  they play in our neighborhoods they're  friends with our kids they pledge  allegiance to our flag they are  Americans in their heart in their minds  in every single way but one on paper so  after several years of advocating and  pushing for a piece of legislation 2012  comes around and we finally have a  program for undocumented youth it gave a  lot of undocumented youth a sense of a  little more freedom and it was hope for  a lot of us this is the first time where  we suddenly felt a sense of belonging  like all the hard work was finally about  to pay off and maybe lead to an  opportunity to access citizenship or a  more long-term solution daca happened  because of grass roots community it is  undocumented folks himself who made  darker happen there's hope because a lot  of people are just realizing and then as  we come together in  like we'll find a way White House  officials tell ABC News that the  president is seriously considering  ending a program that provides legal  status to hundreds of thousands of  undocumented immigrants who came to the  United States as children the move the  president is considering would put  nearly 800,000 undocumented immigrants  in danger of being deported and 2017 is  very hard to process how you're able to  live here you know  you grew up in this country with friends  family with this culture that you used  to and all of the sudden it just the  country just doesn't want to  knowing that Sokka maybe repealed I  think has increase in hyeon's a lot of  people's worries if doc is removed a  hundred thousand people are gonna go  back to a life of anxiety fear and  uncertainty because they can lose their  jobs they might have to withdraw from  certain college programs this could also  mean that they are now that much more  vulnerable to deportation immigration is  a natural occurrence it is gonna happen  and you know we have just we have build  up from it we have seen progress in the  house and just because there's a little  bit of reclining doesn't mean the  progress is gonna stop our main goal is  to how about we deserve we've been in  this country we worked hard we belong  here in the midst of all this I think  it's important to look around and  realize that we have such a big  community of advocates and allies that  really want to see it succeed  immigrants always find a way and where  there is no way we create one and I  believe that that's what's really made  us so strong we need to look to that  same resilience to be able to get  through what lies ahead by any means  necessary we're not going to go anywhere  and we just have to make sure the rest  of the country gets that message  [Music]
 

